WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.233 --> 00:00:00.733
 

00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:02.101
CESAR: When I
started my journey and

00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:04.370
people started calling
me the dog whisperer,

00:00:04.403 --> 00:00:06.205
the only thing I wanted
to do is to teach people

00:00:06.238 --> 00:00:08.207
that there is no such
a thing as a bad dog.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:09.542
WOMAN: Oh, my gosh.

00:00:09.575 --> 00:00:11.478
CESAR: Give me the leash.
She's safer with me.

00:00:12.845 --> 00:00:15.148
Today, I'm still
doing what I love.

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:17.316
But the world has changed.

00:00:17.349 --> 00:00:19.218
(overlapping chatter)

00:00:19.251 --> 00:00:22.455
CESAR: Record dog adoptions
have made many packs complete.

00:00:22.488 --> 00:00:29.162
But now more than ever,
people and dogs still need help.

00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:30.596
WOMAN: We can't live like this.

00:00:30.629 --> 00:00:31.597
CESAR: Yeah.
WOMAN: The problem is me.

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:32.565
MAN: What do I do?

00:00:32.598 --> 00:00:34.434
CESAR: More pooches,
more problems, right?

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:36.102
He needs rehab, like, ASAP.

00:00:36.135 --> 00:00:38.104
So many things
you can fix already.

00:00:38.137 --> 00:00:39.238
Shh.

00:00:39.272 --> 00:00:40.173
MARLON: Sorry, sorry.
CESAR: Ignore the excitement.

00:00:40.206 --> 00:00:41.107
MEGAN: I just don't
want you to get bit.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:43.743
CESAR: Do you know who I am?
MEGAN: Yes.

00:00:43.776 --> 00:00:45.445
CESAR: Together
with my superhero pack,

00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:48.448
our mission is to teach the
world to honor the pack code;

00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:51.217
honesty, integrity, and loyalty.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:53.419
Once you get this, you're
gonna have everything else.

00:00:53.452 --> 00:00:54.653
MAN: Yay!

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:57.223
CESAR: And that's how we create
better humans and better dogs,

00:00:57.256 --> 00:00:59.259
one pack at a time.

00:01:01.827 --> 00:01:03.296
AMANDA: My name
is Amanda Gilmore.

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:05.765
GIL: My name is
MJ Gilmore, but I go by Gil.

00:01:05.798 --> 00:01:07.467
AMANDA: We've
been married two years.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.336
We got married
February 2nd, 2020.

00:01:10.369 --> 00:01:12.538
GIL: Super Bowl Sunday.

00:01:12.571 --> 00:01:13.539
AMANDA: It, but,
it was, it was...

00:01:13.572 --> 00:01:15.408
We didn't plan it
that way intentionally...

00:01:15.441 --> 00:01:16.709
GIL: Yes, she did.
AMANDA: The prize...

00:01:16.742 --> 00:01:18.111
GIL: Yes, she did.

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:19.813
AMANDA: Anyway.

00:01:20.813 --> 00:01:25.284
We got Simba in August, 2020.

00:01:25.317 --> 00:01:26.151
GIL: Mm-hmm.

00:01:26.185 --> 00:01:28.254
AMANDA: So right
dead center of pandemic.

00:01:28.287 --> 00:01:30.289
Simba is a beautiful dog.

00:01:30.322 --> 00:01:32.725
He is like a
Rhodesian Ridgeback mix,

00:01:32.758 --> 00:01:36.162
he has the light eyes,
and he's, you know,

00:01:36.195 --> 00:01:38.231
he's got the beautiful coloring.

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:39.065
GIL: The ears.

00:01:39.098 --> 00:01:40.600
He had one hear up,
one ear down.

00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:41.701
AMANDA: Oh yeah,
he have the, yeah.

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:43.136
GIL: He had the little side,
like I was just like,

00:01:43.169 --> 00:01:45.738
"Oh, you're so adorable."

00:01:45.771 --> 00:01:47.874
And I was like, "That'd be
a great dog for our family."

00:01:47.907 --> 00:01:50.309
Especially our daughter Sienna.

00:01:50.342 --> 00:01:52.145
Say hello to Sienna.

00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:53.679
Say hello to your new sister.

00:01:53.712 --> 00:01:54.813
SIENNA: Oh, hi.

00:01:54.847 --> 00:01:58.618
GIL: When we first got Simba,
he was very playful...

00:02:00.953 --> 00:02:02.722
Very, very open to everything.

00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:05.258
He's very curious,
sniffing around.

00:02:05.291 --> 00:02:07.627
AMANDA: He was very
sweet at the beginning.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:09.629
He was wonderful.

00:02:11.964 --> 00:02:15.501
His behavioral issues started
around six to eight months.

00:02:15.534 --> 00:02:20.640
It started as fear and then it
progressed into more aggressive.

00:02:20.673 --> 00:02:22.675
(growling)

00:02:22.708 --> 00:02:27.380
Simba thinks his job is
to protect us from whatever

00:02:27.413 --> 00:02:29.515
he thinks is scary.

00:02:29.548 --> 00:02:32.185
So if that's another dog
barking through the fence,

00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:33.819
he thinks that's his job.

00:02:33.852 --> 00:02:35.588
Hey, hey. Ssh.

00:02:35.621 --> 00:02:38.324
So when he gets
fixated on something, he,

00:02:38.357 --> 00:02:42.795
he won't let it out of his
sight and he just will stare at

00:02:42.828 --> 00:02:44.230
it super intensely.

00:02:44.263 --> 00:02:47.200
GIL: He'll do a
slight growl at first
and his head is still down.

00:02:47.233 --> 00:02:48.334
(growls)

00:02:48.367 --> 00:02:49.168
AMANDA: Hey.

00:02:49.201 --> 00:02:51.437
GIL: And that's
when we know, um,

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:53.540
that it's a problem.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:55.741
He'll lunge.

00:02:55.775 --> 00:02:57.843
AMANDA: He'll,
he'll lunge at other dogs.

00:02:57.876 --> 00:03:00.479
Uh, we never let him get close
enough to do anything to people

00:03:00.512 --> 00:03:02.982
but you can't have
people talk to him,

00:03:03.015 --> 00:03:06.852
look at him, touch him, do
anything next to him because

00:03:06.885 --> 00:03:09.589
he will be acting
aggressive towards them.

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:12.725
SIENNA: Having a dog like Simba
with behavioral issues kind of

00:03:12.758 --> 00:03:16.562
makes me sad because
it's not the cuddly dog that,

00:03:16.595 --> 00:03:19.899
you know, a lot of people know.

00:03:22.768 --> 00:03:25.004
Two of my cousins
are very scared of him.

00:03:25.037 --> 00:03:27.607
One's like,
"Please, don't kill me."

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:30.610
AMANDA: The tremendous amount
of guilt that comes with a dog

00:03:30.643 --> 00:03:33.446
that is not friendly and
you may not approach that dog,

00:03:33.479 --> 00:03:35.314
and because you feel
like a bad dog owner, right?

00:03:35.347 --> 00:03:36.582
GIL: Yeah. 100%.

00:03:36.615 --> 00:03:39.619
AMANDA: You feel like you can't
help him, that he's always gonna

00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:41.721
be scared and
nervous all the time.

00:03:41.754 --> 00:03:42.922
And it's just,
it's just difficult.

00:03:42.955 --> 00:03:46.859
So having family gatherings and,
and being able to spend time

00:03:46.892 --> 00:03:51.030
with our family is suffering
for like six months with Simba

00:03:51.063 --> 00:03:54.900
not being able to integrate
into our family energy.

00:03:54.933 --> 00:03:56.936
GIL: It, it has
been challenging, um,

00:03:56.969 --> 00:04:00.573
not at the point of we can't
function in life, but it is,

00:04:00.606 --> 00:04:04.010
it's getting closer
and closer to that point.

00:04:04.043 --> 00:04:07.880
AMANDA: The worry in the back
of your mind is always gonna be,

00:04:07.913 --> 00:04:10.016
euthanizing is so harsh,

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:14.754
but it's like if you have a dog
that's that scared all the time,

00:04:14.787 --> 00:04:16.822
I don't know if
there's another choice.

00:04:16.855 --> 00:04:20.393
GIL: Mm-hmm.
AMANDA: Whoa. Sorry.

00:04:20.659 --> 00:04:23.429
GIL: You're good. You're good.

00:04:23.462 --> 00:04:25.431
It's all right.

00:04:25.464 --> 00:04:27.600
We'll figure it out.

00:04:27.633 --> 00:04:28.901
I get it.

00:04:28.934 --> 00:04:31.337
That's why I love you.
This is why I love you.

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:32.772
AMANDA: Let's hope
it doesn't get there.

00:04:32.805 --> 00:04:33.839
GIL: It's not gonna get there.

00:04:33.872 --> 00:04:34.940
AMANDA: Okay.

00:04:34.973 --> 00:04:36.042
GIL: It's gonna be fine,
we're gonna figure it out,

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:37.376
like everything else.

00:04:37.409 --> 00:04:38.644
Uh-huh?

00:04:38.677 --> 00:04:40.613
AMANDA: Yes.

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:46.018
(knocking on door)

00:04:46.051 --> 00:04:47.520
Hi.

00:04:47.553 --> 00:04:48.888
GIL: Hi.
CESAR: Hey, guys.

00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:50.656
Come on out, come on out.

00:04:50.689 --> 00:04:53.059
I wanna hear what's
happening with Simba.

00:04:54.827 --> 00:04:56.095
Tell me, how can I help?

00:04:56.128 --> 00:04:57.663
GIL: Well.
AMANDA: Ooh, well.

00:04:57.696 --> 00:05:00.866
GIL: Our dog Simba,
uh, he's, uh...

00:05:00.899 --> 00:05:02.401
How do you describe Simba?

00:05:02.434 --> 00:05:04.337
AMANDA: He's
like our bodyguard.

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:05.771
GIL: Basically.
AMANDA: Like, all the time.

00:05:05.804 --> 00:05:06.906
GIL: Not in the best way.
CESAR: Yeah.

00:05:06.939 --> 00:05:08.107
AMANDA: Not in a
way you want, right?

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:12.011
He has just developed these
really intense fears for things.

00:05:12.044 --> 00:05:13.045
CESAR: Yeah.

00:05:13.079 --> 00:05:14.680
AMANDA: And we can't
seem to get him out of it.

00:05:14.713 --> 00:05:15.547
CESAR: Help him?
BOTH: Yeah.

00:05:15.581 --> 00:05:17.083
CESAR: All right.
Let's go meet him.

00:05:17.116 --> 00:05:18.417
AMANDA: All right.
Let's do it.

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:19.418
CESAR: I'm ready.

00:05:19.451 --> 00:05:21.854
Gil and Amanda say that
Simba has a lot of fear.

00:05:21.887 --> 00:05:23.823
But when a dog
is overly fearful,

00:05:23.856 --> 00:05:26.926
that usually is a symptom
of a much larger problem.

00:05:26.959 --> 00:05:28.794
Hello. How are you?

00:05:28.827 --> 00:05:30.496
How are you?

00:05:30.529 --> 00:05:32.598
(barking)

00:05:32.631 --> 00:05:34.834
AMANDA: Oh my goodness.

00:05:34.867 --> 00:05:36.068
Ugh, Simba.

00:05:36.102 --> 00:05:39.705
CESAR: That, that is nervous.
GIL: That is, that is Simba.

00:05:39.738 --> 00:05:40.806
AMANDA: Yeah.

00:05:40.839 --> 00:05:42.708
CESAR: Yeah, so you,
so what, if, if he comes out,

00:05:42.741 --> 00:05:44.143
what do you think he'll do?

00:05:44.176 --> 00:05:46.112
AMANDA: I don't let him
out when he's like this.

00:05:46.145 --> 00:05:47.480
GIL: Yeah.
CESAR: Oh.

00:05:47.513 --> 00:05:48.648
AMANDA: This is just.

00:05:48.681 --> 00:05:50.716
GIL: I don't wanna risk it,
trying to avoid a lawsuit.

00:05:50.749 --> 00:05:51.817
AMANDA: Yeah.
CESAR: Right, right.

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:53.553
Can I have a leash?

00:05:55.821 --> 00:05:58.624
GIL: If you say so.
CESAR: Yes, sir.

00:05:58.657 --> 00:06:01.827
Simba is nervous, which leads
to all the aggressive barking.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.797
And his pet parents are also
nervous for what might happen

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:06.832
when Simba exits the crate.

00:06:06.865 --> 00:06:08.467
GIL: Simba.
AMANDA: Sit.

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:09.635
GIL: Hey, hey.

00:06:09.668 --> 00:06:10.803
CESAR: I need to show them
that with the right energy,

00:06:10.836 --> 00:06:14.440
all the hostile behavior they're
expecting can be avoided.

00:06:14.473 --> 00:06:15.941
Pass me the end of the leash.

00:06:15.974 --> 00:06:17.443
GIL: Mm-hmm. Yeah.
You got it?

00:06:17.476 --> 00:06:19.512
CESAR: Yup.
Go sit over there.

00:06:19.545 --> 00:06:21.480
GIL: You, you, you're sure?
CESAR: I'm sure.

00:06:21.513 --> 00:06:22.481
GIL: Okay.

00:06:22.514 --> 00:06:23.983
CESAR: Yeah. Yeah.

00:06:24.016 --> 00:06:27.520
So he's, he's, um, he's safer
in my hands than in your hands

00:06:27.553 --> 00:06:28.721
because you're nervous.

00:06:28.754 --> 00:06:30.523
GIL: Well, I'm nervous
for you, I'm, I'll be...

00:06:30.556 --> 00:06:31.657
CESAR: No, no.
GIL: I'll be fine.

00:06:31.691 --> 00:06:32.925
CESAR: It, it doesn't matter,
it doesn't matter what

00:06:32.958 --> 00:06:34.059
you're nervous from.

00:06:34.092 --> 00:06:34.893
GIL: Yeah. Yeah.

00:06:34.926 --> 00:06:36.061
CESAR: The point
is you're nervous.

00:06:36.094 --> 00:06:37.029
GIL: Yeah, 100%.

00:06:37.062 --> 00:06:38.631
CESAR: If we're
gonna help a nervous dog,

00:06:38.664 --> 00:06:40.933
that dog cannot be
held by a nervous human.

00:06:40.966 --> 00:06:42.768
That just gonna
make things worse.

00:06:42.801 --> 00:06:44.703
If you see that
the dog is nervous,

00:06:44.736 --> 00:06:47.506
eliminate using so
many words so fast.

00:06:47.539 --> 00:06:48.808
"Simba, Simba,
Simba, Simba, Simba."

00:06:48.841 --> 00:06:51.043
Because you're just
gonna add more into it.

00:06:51.076 --> 00:06:51.977
GIL: Okay.

00:06:52.011 --> 00:06:53.546
CESAR: So silence is
actually really healing.

00:06:53.579 --> 00:06:54.680
GIL: Hmm.

00:06:54.713 --> 00:06:55.948
AMANDA: That's
something we don't do.

00:06:55.981 --> 00:06:57.116
GIL: Uh-uh.
SIENNA: Uh-uh.

00:06:57.149 --> 00:06:57.983
AMANDA: Ever.
GIL: Not at all.
SIENNA: Nope. Ever.

00:06:58.017 --> 00:06:59.618
AMANDA: So we're
gonna practice that.

00:06:59.651 --> 00:07:01.754
The silence.

00:07:01.787 --> 00:07:02.988
CESAR: So he, this is good.

00:07:03.021 --> 00:07:05.858
SIENNA: It's
actually quite magical, like,

00:07:05.891 --> 00:07:08.527
see him calm.

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:10.629
Because you're a complete
stranger and he just laid down.

00:07:10.662 --> 00:07:11.897
CESAR: Well, I want
you to have a magical days

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:13.899
every day, Sienna, you know?

00:07:13.932 --> 00:07:16.802
SIENNA: It, it wowed me.
CESAR: Hmm. I wowed Sienna.

00:07:16.835 --> 00:07:17.837
SIENNA: I didn't...
CESAR: That's a big deal.

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:18.971
AMANDA: You did.

00:07:19.005 --> 00:07:21.207
GIL: It feels great to see
that Simba can be calm and

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:24.009
not try to attack every
person or everything that

00:07:24.042 --> 00:07:25.144
comes in the house.

00:07:25.177 --> 00:07:26.512
CESAR: This is more leadership.

00:07:26.545 --> 00:07:28.247
This is leading
the dog to stay calm.

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:29.148
AMANDA: Yeah.

00:07:29.181 --> 00:07:30.149
CESAR: You should
be able to do that,

00:07:30.182 --> 00:07:31.917
but because you don't
trust them, you can't.

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:33.151
AMANDA: Right.

00:07:33.185 --> 00:07:35.521
GIL: It was just shocking all
of us that within five minutes,

00:07:35.554 --> 00:07:39.959
Cesar has so much control
over Simba which is very,

00:07:39.992 --> 00:07:42.995
very unlikely to anybody else.

00:07:43.028 --> 00:07:45.531
CESAR: Well, that would empower
you as much as I can today.

00:07:45.564 --> 00:07:46.932
AMANDA: Yes.
CESAR: So let's go for a walk.

00:07:46.965 --> 00:07:49.134
I do wanna teach you
how to walk around dogs.

00:07:49.167 --> 00:07:50.402
AMANDA: All right. Let's do it.

00:07:50.435 --> 00:07:52.972
CESAR: Bringing Simba outside on
the walk would allow me to both

00:07:53.005 --> 00:07:56.809
assess the dog and the handling
skills of each pet parent.

00:07:56.842 --> 00:07:59.111
So I brought some
handlers and their dogs.

00:07:59.144 --> 00:08:02.081
I need to observe whether both
pet parents' nervousness inside

00:08:02.114 --> 00:08:05.551
is also present on the
walk starting with Gil.

00:08:05.584 --> 00:08:07.920
GIL: Ready? Come on.

00:08:09.555 --> 00:08:10.689
CESAR: One hand.

00:08:10.722 --> 00:08:11.924
GIL: Simba.
CESAR: One hand.

00:08:11.957 --> 00:08:13.692
GIL: Oh.
CESAR: Yeah.

00:08:13.725 --> 00:08:15.227
GIL: But I always
walk him with short leash.

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:16.228
CESAR: Yeah.
Shortened at the ends.

00:08:16.261 --> 00:08:17.362
GIL: Yeah.

00:08:17.396 --> 00:08:18.030
CESAR: You know, it has
to be right next to you and

00:08:18.063 --> 00:08:19.865
he can't just go everywhere.

00:08:19.898 --> 00:08:21.033
GIL: Go, go crazy, yeah.

00:08:21.066 --> 00:08:22.234
CESAR: Go crazy.

00:08:22.267 --> 00:08:24.770
I'm not really seeing any issues
at all when Gil walks Simba.

00:08:24.803 --> 00:08:26.238
AMANDA: Look how
nice he walks with Gil.

00:08:26.271 --> 00:08:28.541
CESAR: Gil doesn't seem
nervous and Simba's only

00:08:28.574 --> 00:08:31.176
a little curious, like,
"Who's this dog next to me?"

00:08:31.209 --> 00:08:33.178
But there is no
aggressive behavior here.

00:08:33.211 --> 00:08:34.179
This is good.

00:08:34.212 --> 00:08:35.247
GIL: Yeah.
CESAR: Good, good.

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:38.918
So now I will observe
Amanda's handling skills.

00:08:49.761 --> 00:08:51.096
AMANDA: Hey.
Hey, hey, hey, hey.

00:08:51.129 --> 00:08:52.831
CESAR: That's not good.

00:08:52.864 --> 00:08:56.068
No, don't let
him pull like that.

00:08:56.101 --> 00:08:57.703
So you see how Simba is moving?

00:08:57.736 --> 00:08:58.604
GIL: Yeah.

00:08:58.637 --> 00:08:59.905
CESAR: He's
choosing what to do versus

00:08:59.938 --> 00:09:01.807
you telling him what to do.

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:03.142
GIL: It never happens with me.

00:09:03.175 --> 00:09:04.910
Whenever he
sees other dog, like,
he'll literally sit down.

00:09:04.943 --> 00:09:07.580
AMANDA: Simba. Ooh.

00:09:07.613 --> 00:09:11.617
CESAR: I'm gonna teach her how
to eliminate this, this 360.

00:09:11.650 --> 00:09:13.920
GIL: Yeah.

00:09:21.126 --> 00:09:23.028
AMANDA: Okay. Come on.

00:09:23.061 --> 00:09:25.030
CESAR: Amanda's energy
is clearly the main cause

00:09:25.063 --> 00:09:26.799
of the aggressive behavior.

00:09:26.832 --> 00:09:29.201
Simba is much more
volatile with her.

00:09:29.234 --> 00:09:31.904
Amanda is nervous because
she doesn't trust her dog and

00:09:31.937 --> 00:09:34.106
she's feeding that nervous
energy through the leash,

00:09:34.139 --> 00:09:37.343
so Simba lashes out
thinking she needs protection.

00:09:37.376 --> 00:09:41.280
What Amanda actually needs is a
lesson in confident dog walking.

00:09:41.313 --> 00:09:42.648
Yeah, too much leash.

00:09:42.681 --> 00:09:43.549
AMANDA: Too much?

00:09:43.582 --> 00:09:45.150
CESAR: So you're
giving him an option.

00:09:45.183 --> 00:09:46.852
Shorten out the option.

00:09:46.885 --> 00:09:48.287
Look, shorten, not tense.

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:49.822
AMANDA: Okay.
CESAR: You walk with me.

00:09:49.855 --> 00:09:51.624
AMANDA: Okay.

00:09:52.958 --> 00:09:53.926
(growls)

00:09:53.959 --> 00:09:55.794
Hey. Ssh. Hey.

00:09:55.827 --> 00:09:57.663
CESAR: Tsch.

00:09:57.696 --> 00:09:58.897
Are you nervous?

00:09:58.931 --> 00:10:03.369
AMANDA: Yes, I'm trying to...
CESAR: Relax. Try it again.

00:10:09.141 --> 00:10:10.743
Stay calm, stay confident.

00:10:10.776 --> 00:10:13.812
AMANDA: Okay.

00:10:13.845 --> 00:10:15.047
CESAR: You're getting tense.

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:17.349
AMANDA: Tsch.

00:10:17.382 --> 00:10:19.051
Ow! Hey!

00:10:19.084 --> 00:10:20.319
CESAR: Did he bite?
AMANDA: Yeah, he did.

00:10:23.689 --> 00:10:25.391
CESAR: Pass him to me.

00:10:25.424 --> 00:10:27.092
Sit.

00:10:27.125 --> 00:10:29.895
Tsch. You okay?

00:10:29.928 --> 00:10:31.063
AMANDA: Yeah.

00:10:31.096 --> 00:10:32.831
CESAR: Amanda may
not be injured but

00:10:32.864 --> 00:10:35.134
every time a
dog bites, it's serious.

00:10:35.167 --> 00:10:37.703
Even though she shortened
the leash like instructed,

00:10:37.736 --> 00:10:40.739
she was still nervous and Simba
felt that through the leash.

00:10:40.772 --> 00:10:43.409
That energy buildup
with no way of releasing it,

00:10:43.442 --> 00:10:46.078
so he redirected on
Amanda with a bite.

00:10:46.111 --> 00:10:47.813
AMANDA: Ow.

00:10:47.846 --> 00:10:48.981
CESAR: Are you injured?

00:10:49.014 --> 00:10:51.050
AMANDA: No, no, I'm not, it,
it didn't hurt that much.

00:10:51.083 --> 00:10:52.051
GIL: Okay.
AMANDA: I just...

00:10:52.084 --> 00:10:52.852
GIL: What happened?

00:10:52.885 --> 00:10:54.353
AMANDA: I just
felt like failure.

00:10:54.386 --> 00:10:55.921
GIL: You're not.

00:10:55.954 --> 00:10:56.855
What do you always tell me?

00:10:56.889 --> 00:10:58.390
It's not gonna be
an overnight process.

00:10:58.423 --> 00:10:59.892
This is a marathon,
not a sprint.

00:10:59.925 --> 00:11:00.793
AMANDA: I got...
GIL: You got this, baby.

00:11:00.826 --> 00:11:01.861
AMANDA: I got it.
GIL: It's all right.

00:11:01.894 --> 00:11:02.928
AMANDA: I got it, I got it.
GIL: It's all right.

00:11:02.961 --> 00:11:04.063
I promise you.

00:11:04.096 --> 00:11:06.265
AMANDA: I just,
I can't, I don't know.

00:11:06.298 --> 00:11:11.103
I'm like, you know,
scared because I'm, like,

00:11:11.136 --> 00:11:13.172
I hope I could do it.

00:11:13.205 --> 00:11:17.776
But I just, that
was not my moment.

00:11:17.809 --> 00:11:21.447
But I hope that I can get there.

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:23.349
I think I can.

00:11:25.817 --> 00:11:27.920
It, it was just, it was just a
scary moment for me because I,

00:11:27.953 --> 00:11:29.388
you know, while he wasn't,

00:11:29.421 --> 00:11:31.957
I don't think trying
to hurt me intentionally,

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:33.425
it's just, you just
don't have control.

00:11:33.458 --> 00:11:36.361
So it just feels
like failure and then

00:11:36.394 --> 00:11:38.964
the crying releases energy.

00:11:38.997 --> 00:11:39.965
It's always been
that way for me,

00:11:39.998 --> 00:11:43.802
crying helps suggest,
get it out, and then,

00:11:43.835 --> 00:11:45.738
and then you can move past it.

00:11:45.771 --> 00:11:47.773
CESAR: The failure
is not a failure,

00:11:47.806 --> 00:11:49.908
it's a learning
experience because,

00:11:49.941 --> 00:11:52.778
you know, this
is your first time.

00:11:52.811 --> 00:11:55.380
Amanda feels like a failure
but the true problem here is

00:11:55.413 --> 00:11:58.016
a breakdown in trust.

00:11:58.049 --> 00:12:00.853
Simba doesn't trust that
Amanda can protect herself,

00:12:00.886 --> 00:12:03.789
so he gets
fiercely overprotective.

00:12:03.822 --> 00:12:07.326
Then Amanda doesn't trust Simba
around other dogs or people.

00:12:09.261 --> 00:12:11.396
Amanda's frustration
doesn't surprise me.

00:12:11.429 --> 00:12:14.166
Pet parents become overwhelmed
with frustration because

00:12:14.199 --> 00:12:16.769
they feel they're
unable to trust their dog and

00:12:16.802 --> 00:12:20.005
they feel that every odd
burst is another step closer to

00:12:20.038 --> 00:12:22.241
having to put the dog down.

00:12:22.274 --> 00:12:24.076
I believe there
is hope for Simba,

00:12:24.109 --> 00:12:27.346
but this entire rehabilitation
depends on getting him and

00:12:27.379 --> 00:12:30.015
Amanda to the DPC
to begin our work.

00:12:30.048 --> 00:12:32.484
The thing is we
found the problem,

00:12:32.517 --> 00:12:34.219
but it's, it's a process.

00:12:34.252 --> 00:12:35.453
AMANDA: Yeah.

00:12:35.487 --> 00:12:37.389
CESAR: So I'm gonna invite you
to my ranch so you can build

00:12:37.422 --> 00:12:38.857
the trust you're missing.

00:12:38.890 --> 00:12:39.858
Let's work on that.

00:12:39.891 --> 00:12:41.227
AMANDA: Yes.

00:12:44.362 --> 00:12:46.899
NARRATOR: The next day, Cesar is
back on the road to help another

00:12:46.932 --> 00:12:51.136
 pandemic rescue, whose anxious
 and destructive behavior holds

00:12:51.169 --> 00:12:53.773
 the lives of his
 pet parents' hostage.

00:13:00.545 --> 00:13:03.415
AMANDA: Okay. Come on.

00:13:04.516 --> 00:13:06.151
My name's Amanda.

00:13:06.184 --> 00:13:07.186
JAY: And I'm Jay.

00:13:07.219 --> 00:13:08.954
I'm a special education teacher

00:13:08.987 --> 00:13:11.924
and I work with high school
students with special needs.

00:13:11.957 --> 00:13:16.829
AMANDA: And I recently
just graduated with
my master's degree

00:13:16.862 --> 00:13:19.565
in clinical psychology
and I've just started

00:13:19.598 --> 00:13:21.233
my therapy practice.

00:13:21.266 --> 00:13:25.838
We have a pup named Otto who
we adopted a few months back.

00:13:25.871 --> 00:13:28.874
We think Otto is
about nine months.

00:13:28.907 --> 00:13:31.043
JAY: He's a mini schnauzer mix.

00:13:31.076 --> 00:13:33.879
AMANDA: He had this look that
was just so sweet and he licked

00:13:33.912 --> 00:13:35.981
our faces and jumped
in our car within like...

00:13:36.014 --> 00:13:37.182
JAY: Mm-hmm.
AMANDA: 30 seconds.

00:13:37.215 --> 00:13:38.317
So he kind of adopted us.

00:13:38.350 --> 00:13:39.952
JAY: Yeah.

00:13:40.986 --> 00:13:44.056
Sit. Good boy, stay.

00:13:44.089 --> 00:13:46.625
Good boy. All right.

00:13:46.658 --> 00:13:52.064
Otto seems like a wise old
man in like a baby's body.

00:13:52.097 --> 00:13:54.633
AMANDA: He is!
He even has the beard for it.

00:13:54.666 --> 00:13:55.701
JAY: Yeah, he...

00:13:55.734 --> 00:13:56.835
AMANDA: Like, people
don't realize he's a puppy.

00:13:56.868 --> 00:13:59.571
JAY: Yeah, he reminds of
like little Michael J. Fox.

00:13:59.604 --> 00:14:01.072
You know what I mean?

00:14:01.106 --> 00:14:02.441
AMANDA: We always talk
about what his voice would
be like if he had one.

00:14:02.474 --> 00:14:03.876
JAY: Yeah. He's Marty McFly.

00:14:03.909 --> 00:14:05.411
AMANDA: Yeah, Marty McFly.

00:14:06.344 --> 00:14:07.512
We fed him, right?

00:14:07.545 --> 00:14:09.581
JAY: Yeah, twice today.

00:14:09.614 --> 00:14:12.484
AMANDA: Since we got
Otto during a pandemic,

00:14:12.517 --> 00:14:15.354
he has been home
with us all the time.

00:14:15.387 --> 00:14:17.957
It's okay, bubba, it's okay.

00:14:19.190 --> 00:14:20.659
He'll be right,
he'll be right back.

00:14:20.692 --> 00:14:22.194
Come on.

00:14:22.227 --> 00:14:24.997
He's adorable and
he's fluffy and cuddly but

00:14:25.030 --> 00:14:28.066
we have become
hostages in our own home.

00:14:28.099 --> 00:14:29.534
He's our captor at this point.

00:14:29.567 --> 00:14:32.271
Honey, he'll be right back.

00:14:32.304 --> 00:14:34.172
We can't leave him alone.

00:14:34.205 --> 00:14:36.342
He doesn't know
how to self soothe.

00:14:36.575 --> 00:14:38.310
(scratching)

00:14:38.343 --> 00:14:42.081
JAY: His way of coping with
things is scratching, biting.

00:14:43.381 --> 00:14:44.349
I think he just doesn't know...

00:14:44.382 --> 00:14:45.283
AMANDA: Destructive.

00:14:45.317 --> 00:14:46.084
JAY: What to do with
himself when he's alone.

00:14:46.117 --> 00:14:46.751
AMANDA: He's
kind of destructive.

00:14:46.785 --> 00:14:48.353
JAY: Yeah.
AMANDA: Yeah.

00:14:48.386 --> 00:14:51.990
Otto. What were you doing?

00:14:52.023 --> 00:14:53.959
Mommy was working.

00:14:53.992 --> 00:14:59.431
Otto makes our lifestyle,
or mine particularly difficult

00:14:59.464 --> 00:15:03.302
because I've started a practice
and because of the pandemic

00:15:03.335 --> 00:15:05.404
I'm still working virtually.

00:15:05.437 --> 00:15:07.406
(whines)

00:15:07.439 --> 00:15:09.675
Down. Get down.

00:15:09.708 --> 00:15:12.244
And so now that I'm in session,

00:15:12.277 --> 00:15:14.613
he's jumping on me,
he wants my attention,

00:15:14.646 --> 00:15:16.214
he wants to sit on my lap.

00:15:16.247 --> 00:15:18.717
And I can't leave him in
another room or a crate because

00:15:18.750 --> 00:15:20.920
he's barking all the time.

00:15:23.121 --> 00:15:25.357
It's getting to the point
where he's kind of sabotaging

00:15:25.390 --> 00:15:28.093
my career before it's
even getting started because

00:15:28.126 --> 00:15:30.329
the behavior's gotten worse.

00:15:30.362 --> 00:15:32.531
JAY: This is our first
dog together as a couple.

00:15:32.564 --> 00:15:35.534
And we don't know
totally what we're doing.

00:15:35.567 --> 00:15:40.306
We need our life back
and I think Cesar's our
only answer at this point.

00:15:50.148 --> 00:15:51.350
(knocking on door)

00:15:51.383 --> 00:15:53.251
AMANDA: Otto, sit.

00:15:53.284 --> 00:15:56.221
Good boy. Stay.

00:15:56.254 --> 00:15:57.556
Hi.

00:15:57.589 --> 00:15:58.724
CESAR: How are you?
JAY: Hi, how are you?

00:15:58.757 --> 00:16:00.092
AMANDA: Good. How are you?
CESAR: Come on out. Come on out.

00:16:00.125 --> 00:16:01.560
Looking forward to helping you.

00:16:01.593 --> 00:16:02.761
AMANDA: Thank you. We need it.

00:16:02.794 --> 00:16:05.197
JAY: For sure, so, we got a
nine-month-old pup named Otto...

00:16:05.230 --> 00:16:06.198
CESAR: Okay.
JAY: But...

00:16:06.231 --> 00:16:07.132
(barks)

00:16:07.166 --> 00:16:09.234
CESAR: Oh. There he is.
JAY: There he goes.

00:16:09.267 --> 00:16:11.236
CESAR: That is an excited bark.

00:16:11.269 --> 00:16:14.339
As soon as Jay and Amanda
step outside the house,

00:16:14.372 --> 00:16:17.009
Otto barked to let them
know he doesn't want to be

00:16:17.042 --> 00:16:18.410
separated from them.

00:16:18.443 --> 00:16:20.779
I need to find out
what's the cause of this.

00:16:20.812 --> 00:16:25.584
AMANDA: Because of
the pandemic I've been
working at home and it's,

00:16:25.617 --> 00:16:29.521
his behavior is
kind of sabotaging
the start of my career.

00:16:29.554 --> 00:16:32.457
CESAR: It's important
for the humans to know

00:16:32.490 --> 00:16:34.393
how they create instability.

00:16:34.426 --> 00:16:37.329
If you spend time with a
dog and you allow the dog to

00:16:37.362 --> 00:16:41.700
follow you around 24/7, that
creates codependency, right?

00:16:41.733 --> 00:16:43.068
JAY: Right.
AMANDA: Mm-hmm.

00:16:43.101 --> 00:16:45.203
CESAR: So the dog has
to learn the state of
mind of calm surrender.

00:16:45.236 --> 00:16:46.371
AMANDA: Mm-hmm.

00:16:46.404 --> 00:16:47.539
JAY: He has no
calm surrender yet.

00:16:47.572 --> 00:16:49.040
AMANDA: None.

00:16:49.074 --> 00:16:50.509
CESAR: What I would
like to see right now is
the ritual how you leave.

00:16:50.542 --> 00:16:52.210
AMANDA: Yeah.
This will be interesting
for you to see how this goes.

00:16:52.243 --> 00:16:53.044
JAY: Yeah. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:16:53.077 --> 00:16:53.878
CESAR: I want to
see you in action.

00:16:53.912 --> 00:16:55.480
JAY: Here we go.
AMANDA: Yes, okay.

00:16:55.513 --> 00:16:56.381
CESAR: All right.
AMANDA: Ready?

00:16:56.414 --> 00:16:57.482
JAY: Yeah. Let's do it.
AMANDA: All right.

00:16:57.515 --> 00:16:58.683
JAY: Let's do it.

00:16:58.716 --> 00:17:00.619
CESAR: It seems like Otto
suffers from what is known as

00:17:00.652 --> 00:17:03.755
separation anxiety, which is
a condition where a dog becomes

00:17:03.788 --> 00:17:07.492
anxious when their pack or
human is no longer near them.

00:17:07.525 --> 00:17:09.494
It's fairly common,
but if left unchecked,

00:17:09.527 --> 00:17:12.831
it can lead to destructive
behavior and self-harm.

00:17:12.864 --> 00:17:15.367
To assess the cause of
Otto's separation anxiety,

00:17:15.400 --> 00:17:17.736
I need to observe what
mental state the dog is in

00:17:17.769 --> 00:17:20.072
before the parents
leave him alone.

00:17:20.105 --> 00:17:22.307
Super ready.

00:17:23.675 --> 00:17:24.843
AMANDA: Hi, bud.
JAY: Hey, buddy.

00:17:24.876 --> 00:17:27.245
AMANDA: Hey, buddy.
JAY: What's up, dude?

00:17:27.278 --> 00:17:28.480
What's up, dude?

00:17:28.513 --> 00:17:29.481
AMANDA: What
are we doing today?

00:17:29.514 --> 00:17:30.482
JAY: What's up, dude?

00:17:30.515 --> 00:17:31.316
CESAR: It's already cartoonish.

00:17:31.349 --> 00:17:32.484
 AMANDA (over video):
 Come here.

00:17:32.517 --> 00:17:33.452
CESAR: Ooh.

00:17:33.485 --> 00:17:34.486
 AMANDA (over video):
 Give me some love.

00:17:34.519 --> 00:17:35.587
CESAR: Lot of baby talk.

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:38.356
AMANDA: It's almost bedtime,
are you ready for bed?

00:17:38.389 --> 00:17:40.258
Are you ready for bed?

00:17:40.291 --> 00:17:41.693
It's getting dark out.

00:17:41.726 --> 00:17:42.627
Go to sleep.

00:17:42.661 --> 00:17:44.729
CESAR: Why are they
putting the curtain?

00:17:44.762 --> 00:17:48.633
JAY: If we wanna go do
anything past like 5:00,

00:17:48.666 --> 00:17:54.473
we have to, sort of, coordinate
or set up this scenario like

00:17:54.506 --> 00:17:57.109
we're all going to bed, so
Otto thinks it's like bedtime.

00:17:57.142 --> 00:17:59.377
So he has no idea that
we're actually leaving.

00:17:59.410 --> 00:18:01.580
CESAR: I guess they're
created fake nighttime?

00:18:01.613 --> 00:18:05.750
JAY: We'll go so far as to hang
like blankets over the windows

00:18:05.783 --> 00:18:07.619
to actually make it look
darker than it really is,

00:18:07.652 --> 00:18:10.555
just to give the illusion
that it's actually bedtime.

00:18:10.588 --> 00:18:12.424
AMANDA: We might
need to go to bed.

00:18:12.457 --> 00:18:15.227
JAY: Oh, bedtime.
AMANDA: Bedtime.

00:18:15.260 --> 00:18:17.863
CESAR: That's a production.

00:18:17.896 --> 00:18:19.731
AMANDA: Time for sleep.

00:18:19.764 --> 00:18:22.434
CESAR: They talk the whole time?

00:18:22.467 --> 00:18:23.702
JAY: All right, good night, OT.

00:18:23.735 --> 00:18:25.671
AMANDA: Night.

00:18:26.704 --> 00:18:28.273
CESAR: I'm in shock right now.

00:18:31.943 --> 00:18:34.679
♪ ♪

00:18:34.712 --> 00:18:37.449
CESAR: And now they're waking.

00:18:37.482 --> 00:18:39.551
Oh, this is how they leave?

00:18:42.453 --> 00:18:43.622
Wow.

00:18:43.655 --> 00:18:45.891
I never seen anything like this.

00:18:45.924 --> 00:18:49.494
Jay and Amanda put on this
elaborate production so they can

00:18:49.527 --> 00:18:52.564
sneak out of the room
without Otto becoming anxious.

00:18:52.597 --> 00:18:54.700
I mean, it's insane.

00:19:04.342 --> 00:19:05.176
JAY: Babe.

00:19:05.210 --> 00:19:06.611
CESAR: You guys
do that to get out?

00:19:06.644 --> 00:19:07.746
AMANDA: Oh, yeah. Oops.

00:19:07.779 --> 00:19:08.914
(rattling)

00:19:10.748 --> 00:19:11.783
AMANDA: Every time.

00:19:11.816 --> 00:19:12.917
CESAR: But that's
like a, like a theater, like.

00:19:12.950 --> 00:19:14.853
AMANDA: Yeah, it takes
an extra, like, half an hour.

00:19:14.886 --> 00:19:15.987
Every time we have
to leave the house.

00:19:16.021 --> 00:19:18.823
CESAR: I mean, it's a big
production to put curtains

00:19:18.856 --> 00:19:20.225
over a curtain.

00:19:20.258 --> 00:19:21.960
And then pretend
you're gonna go to sleep.

00:19:21.993 --> 00:19:25.297
And the dog is like,
number one, they're lying.

00:19:25.330 --> 00:19:28.266
Number two, they just left.

00:19:28.299 --> 00:19:29.601
They're not even here.

00:19:29.634 --> 00:19:30.602
(whines)

00:19:30.635 --> 00:19:31.770
You see what I mean?

00:19:31.803 --> 00:19:32.904
You're like, how
do you fool a dog?

00:19:32.937 --> 00:19:34.306
He knows, you know?

00:19:34.339 --> 00:19:35.674
The energy is pretending.

00:19:35.707 --> 00:19:36.875
You're lying.

00:19:36.908 --> 00:19:39.711
In order for you to help,
they have to see the reality.

00:19:39.744 --> 00:19:41.279
JAY: It's like we
think we're fooling him.

00:19:41.312 --> 00:19:43.215
But we know that he
knows we're not fooling him.

00:19:43.248 --> 00:19:44.249
You know?

00:19:44.283 --> 00:19:45.417
CESAR: But think about it,
think about it, how,

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:46.251
how they perceive it.

00:19:46.284 --> 00:19:48.453
The reality is
actually a much simpler.

00:19:48.486 --> 00:19:50.589
Stay quiet. We need distance.

00:19:50.622 --> 00:19:52.224
AMANDA: Mm-hmm.
JAY: Yeah.

00:19:52.257 --> 00:19:53.959
CESAR: But he doesn't
know the calm surrender in
distance makes you happy yet.

00:19:53.992 --> 00:19:55.594
JAY: Yeah. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:19:55.627 --> 00:19:56.861
CESAR: Because you're
so tentative about it.

00:19:56.894 --> 00:19:57.963
JAY: Yeah, yeah.
AMANDA: Yeah.

00:19:57.996 --> 00:19:59.197
CESAR: "It's okay."

00:19:59.231 --> 00:20:00.966
You're, I mean, talking the
whole time, like, "It's okay."

00:20:00.999 --> 00:20:01.800
JAY: Yeah.

00:20:01.833 --> 00:20:03.368
CESAR: "Baby, we
love you so much."

